      "notes": "India's National Capital Region as the NCR Planning Board defines it: all of Delhi, 14 districts of Haryana, 8 of Uttar Pradesh and Alwar and Bharatpur in Rajasthan (about 55,000 km²; Rajasthan's newer districts carved from those two are inside it). The bare short name NCR is read as this one; Canada's capital region is named in full. India has no openly licensed district polygons, so districts are drawn as the PIN code areas the India Post directory files under them (a PIN area's district is the one most of its offices name); PIN areas can cross district lines, so the outline is the postal approximation of the districts, not their legal boundary."
